    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d101">

                <pb facs="0209" n="121" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv02/pg121"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.00/403: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Denmark</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Grant-Smith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Acting Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Copenhagen</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1918-12-16T19:00:00+01:00"
                            >December 16, 1918—7 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:36 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3309. From a [reliable] German source it is reported that political situation in
                    Germany has improved. The National Assembly seems now assured. It will be
                    composed of 60 percent Socialist representatives, the Majority Socialist
                    preponderating so that the Government will rest on a coalition of the two
                    Socialist parties or a coalition between the Majority Socialist and the Radical
                    bourgeois elements. The latter seems more probable in view of the growing
                    disunity between the two Socialistic parties. Count Bernstorff<anchor n="17"
                        xml:id="d101-f1"/> will go as one of the German representatives to the Peace
                    Conference. He told a friend <pb facs="0210" n="122" xml:id="pg122"/> recently
                    that upon his arrival he would communicate with Colonel House personally and say
                    “I have a letter from you dated shortly before I left America expressing such
                    and such opinions about me; have you changed those opinions?” If the answer is
                    yes, Bernstorff stated that he would be taken ill and leave for Berlin within 24
                    hours.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grant-Smith</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d101-f1" n="17" xml:id="d101-f1-content">
                <p>Former German Ambassador in the United States.</p>
            </note>
